@startuml
title PinchResolver - give away 1-cell-wide axis pinches instead of eroding them forever

start
:Scan every cell in the region\n(after all 4 existing cleanup passes\nhave already run to their own fixed point);

if (cell is 1-wide along X\n(both east/west neighbors differ)\nOR 1-wide along Z\n(both north/south neighbors differ),\nAND has 1+ same-biome neighbor\non the other axis?) then (no)
  :Not a pinch - skip;
else (yes)
  :Candidate biomes = the flanking\nbiome(s) on the pinched axis\n(1 or 2 distinct candidates);
  while (candidates left to try,\nnot yet resolved?) is (yes)
    :Pick next candidate biome;
    if (candidate legal at this cell\nagainst ALL its neighbors?) then (no)
    else (yes)
      if (losing this cell keeps the\nCURRENT biome's component connected\nAND at/above its own declared\nminimum size?\n(reuse SeparatorWidener.stealingPreserves\nDonorConnectivity + SeparatorOrganicizer\n.donorComponentSizeWithout/minCellsFor)) then (no)
      else (yes)
        :Reassign cell to candidate biome;
        :Resolved - stop trying candidates\nfor this cell;
      endif
    endif
  endwhile (no more candidates / resolved)
  if (still unresolved?) then (yes)
    :Leave the cell as-is - same\n"never force" rule as the rest\nof the pipeline;
  else (no)
  endif
endif

stop

note right
  Targets a specific measured failure mode, not a
  generic "make PatchOpener stricter" fix: a cell can
  be part of a genuine 2x2 same-biome square along ONE
  axis (so PatchOpener's isCore() correctly never
  erodes it) while still being exactly 1-wide on the
  OTHER axis - a tab sticking sideways off a solid
  block, backed by 3+ Moore-neighbors from that block
  alone. PatchOpener's erode step actually DOES flag
  this cell as non-core every pass (no 2x2 square
  touches it), but its re-dilation step reclaims it
  right back via the same Moore-neighbor-count check
  (even at the stricter +1 threshold) because that
  count is satisfied entirely by the block behind it,
  with zero awareness of the perpendicular axis - erode
  and reclaim repeat identically forever, and the
  fixed-point loop just stops (no progress != no pinch).

  Confirmed on real data (seed 42, macro-cell
  (-24,-24)): savanna cell with 3 Moore-neighbors from
  a solid savanna block to the north, flanked directly
  east/west by plains/desert - exactly this shape.

  This pass runs ONCE, after the existing 4 passes
  settle, and does the opposite of erosion: instead of
  removing the cell and hoping re-dilation does better,
  it directly asks "can one of the two biomes actually
  pinching this cell legally and safely absorb it
  instead?" - a bounded, single-cell decision reusing
  already-proven safety primitives (donor connectivity,
  donor minimum size), never a new kind of check.
end note
@enduml
